## Coding Style Guidelines

### General Principles

- Clean, readable, maintainable code above all
- No over-engineering for single-use or simple code
- No unnecessary abstractions, inheritance, or method overloading for single-use code
- No repeated code blocks - DRY
- Always look at existing implementation first; make the smallest diff possible
- No non-Unicode characters (anything that can't be typed on a normal keyboard) in source code, comments, or docstrings

### Structure

| Context        | Rule                                                |
| -------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| `.ipynb` files | All imports at the top of the file                  |
| New files      | Check existing patterns before introducing new ones |
| Diffs          | Fewest lines changed that achieves the goal         |

### General writing principles

- Always use **NumPy** style docstrings
- Scope: extensive — cover **what**, **why**, **what not to do**, **how**
- No meta-commentary ("updated function", "as requested", "improved version")
- Add lower case comments everywhere, liberally - assume future reader has no context
- Comments in royal first person (remove the I, me, am , we from the comments. Imperative tone)
- No meta-commentary from the assistant
- Type hints are required on all function signatures (params + return type)
- Use `print` statements and logging wherever useful for tracing execution
- Prefer `logging` over `print` in library/module code; either is fine in notebooks/scripts
- Always use f-string formatting over %s formatting
- When editing code, NEVER remove existing module level docstrings. Edit where necessary but only minimally. Never remove user written docstrings.

### Formatting and linting

- Run `ruff` or `black` on every file where applicable, whichever is available
- `ruff` preferred when both are present


## Git Commit Discipline

### Format

```
<type>[(<scope>)][!]: <subject>

[body]

[footer(s)]
```

`!` = breaking change (SemVer MAJOR). Scope: short lowercase (`api`, `auth`, `db`, `cli`, `ui`, `config`).

### Types

| Type       | When to use                                                             | SemVer |
| ---------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------ |
| `feat`     | New feature or behavior                                                 | MINOR  |
| `fix`      | Bug fix                                                                 | PATCH  |
| `refactor` | No bug fix, no feature                                                  | —      |
| `perf`     | Performance improvement                                                 | PATCH  |
| `test`     | Adding or correcting tests                                              | —      |
| `docs`     | Documentation only                                                      | —      |
| `style`    | Formatting, whitespace — no logic change                                | —      |
| `build`    | Build system or dependency changes                                      | —      |
| `ci`       | CI config changes                                                       | —      |
| `chore`    | Tooling, deps, config — no production code                              | —      |
| `agent`    | Changes to claude or other coding agents configuration files or folders | —      |
| `revert`   | Revert a prior commit (ref SHA in body)                                 | varies |

### Subject Line (hard rules)

- Imperative mood: "add", "fix", "remove" — not "added", "fixes"
- No capital after the colon
- No trailing period
- 50 characters max
- No filler words ("just", "some", "various", "minor")

### Body

- Blank line after subject; wrap at 72 chars
- Explain **why**, not what; use bullets for multi-point rationale
- Only include when subject is insufficient
- Even body is optional if change is not that significant

### Footer

- `BREAKING CHANGE: <desc>` — mandatory for any breaking change
- `Refs: #42` — non-closing issue references

**DO NOT ADD AI MODEL CO-AUTHOR GIT COMMIT ATTRIBUTION. IT IS AUTOMATED AND WILL BE ADDED APPROPRIATELY AND PROGRAMMATICALLY**

### Atomic Commit Rules (non-negotiable)

- One logical change per commit — one fix, one feature slice, one refactor. Changes must be committed at the file level. File chunks, hunks, patches SHOULD NOT be committed.
- All files in the commit must belong to that one logical change
- Tests travel with the change they cover — they only go in a seperate commit if changes for tests are larger than the limits mentioned below
- Documentation changes may go in a separate `docs:` commit
- Never commit a broken state (every commit must pass CI)
- If commit message needs "and" -> split the commit
- If using GitHub issues, each commit covers at most one issue
- If > 7 files or > 100 lines changed -> split into different commits (hard limit)

### Never Commit

- Secrets, API keys, tokens
- `.DS_Store` and OS metadata
- Generated files not tracked by project convention
- Lock file changes from unrelated dep drift
- Reformatting of files you didn't logically touch
- Multiple unrelated fixes bundled as "misc fixes"

